content.text:
Now replace the btnScan click handler with: click -> fileInput.click(); change -> runOcr(file); cancel -> fallback demo mode. Also handle the case where file input doesn't fire 'cancel' (older browsers): use focus-based fallback? Simpler: listen for 'change' and 'cancel'. If neither, nothing happens — acceptable but a bit dead-end. Add a fallback: after clicking, use setTimeout to detect if no file chosen... risky (file dialog takes arbitrary time). Better: also allow clicking the scan area itself? Keep simple: add 'cancel' event (supported in Chrome 113+, Safari 16.4+, Firefox 113+). Plus a fallback via window focus check: after fileInput.click(), listen for window focus; on focus, wait 300ms and if no files, run demo mode. That's a common heuristic. But could double-fire with 'cancel'. Guard with state.scanning / a flag.

Let me implement:

```js
el.btnScan.addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (state.scanning) return;
  if (state.docScanned) { resetScanUI(); return; }
  openCapture();
});

function openCapture() {
  let settled = false;
  const onSettle = (file) => { if (settled) return; settled = true; cleanup(); runOcr(file); };
  ...
}
```

Hmm, careful: 'cancel' fires then window focus also fires → guard with settled flag.

Implementation:

```js
function openCapture() {
  let settled = false;

  const finish = (file) => {
    if (settled) return;
    settled = true;
    el.fileInput.removeEventListener('change', onChange);
    el.fileInput.removeEventListener('cancel', onCancel);
    window.removeEventListener('focus', onFocus);
    runOcr(file);
  };

  function onChange() {
    const f = el.fileInput.files && el.fileInput.files[0];
    if (f) finish(f);
  }
  function onCancel() { finish(null); }   // usuário cancelou → modo demo
  function onFocus() {
    // Fallback para navegadores sem o evento 'cancel': ao voltar o foco à
    // janela, damos 400ms e consideramos "sem arquivo" (demo).
    setTimeout(() => finish(el.fileInput.files[0] || null), 400);
  }

  el.fileInput.addEventListener('change', onChange);
  el.fileInput.addEventListener('cancel', onCancel);
  window.addEventListener('focus', onFocus);

  el.fileInput.click();
}
```

Wait: onFocus with files[0] — if change already handled it, settled guard handles. But if user picks a file, 'change' fires before/around focus. Race: focus fires first with files already set? Then finish(files[0]) with400ms delay — change likely fires within that window and finish(f) sets settled... actually change would call finish first if it fires first. Either way file is captured. But if focus timer fires at 400ms and files has the file, fine.

Edge: on desktop, when file dialog opens, window loses focus; on close regains focus → fallback works. Good.

Edge: mobile camera capture — returning may take longer than 400ms to set files? 'change' fires when dialog closes. If focus event fires at close and files not yet populated → we'd finish(null) incorrectly. Increase to 700ms? Risk still. Better: in onFocus fallback, check `el.fileInput.files.length` after 700ms; if 0, finish(null). If change fires later than that... rare. Also if user actually picked a file late, onChange still guarded by settled → ignored. To reduce risk, only use focus fallback when 'cancel' event is unsupported. Feature-detect:

```js
const supportsCancel = 'oncancel' in el.fileInput;
if (!supportsCancel) window.addEventListener('focus', onFocus);
```

That's cleaner: modern browsers use 'cancel'; old ones use focus heuristic. Good.

Also, if user picks a file but 'cancel' unsupported and focus fires before change... 700ms buffer.

Now runOcr(file): loading UI, preview, ocrService.scan(file), fill, etc. And in ocrService, keep2s demo (comment says real endpoint). Also mark scanning flag.

Also update resetScanUI to clear preview + input value + scanning.

Also button label: since button now opens capture, label "📷 Escanear documento" stays; after scan → "🔄 Escanear novamente".

Let's write the replacement for section 7.

